e {641, 1465, 1583} (step_03) while 1293's only in-neighbours are {28, 1438} (step_04); these sets are disjoint, so no 2-hop time-respecting chain can exist and any cascade would need at least 3 hops routed through hubs. The 2-hop static neighbourhood (step_02) shows that such a static connection does exist mainly through very high-degree hubs (e.g. 28, 1583), which is exactly the confound the task warns about: static reachability via hubs is not evidence of ordered, \u2264600-unit-gap relays. Because I obtained no timestamp-level evidence for any candidate relay chain, I cannot confirm or refute a genuine time-respecting cascade; the structural bottleneck makes it unlikely but unproven.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 12596, "total_output_tokens": 1995, "total_tokens": 14591, "total_latency_ms": 30291, "llm_calls": 3, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}